93. Updating views
Simple views can be updated, but complex views containing joins or aggregates usually cannot
If a view is based on a single table, data can often be updated through the view.

Simple Views:- Created from a single base table without any aggregation functions. They generally permit direct data modification (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) through the view.
Complex Views:- Built from multiple tables using JOIN conditions, subqueries, or groupings (GROUP BY). They typically do not support direct data modifications due to their structural complexity.

UPDATE emp_view
SET department='Finance'
WHERE emp_id=1;

Output
Before:
emp_id	department
1	IT

After:
emp_id	department
1	Finance

94. Stored procedures
A stored procedure is a collection of SQL statements stored in the database and executed when called.
Why Do We Use Procedures?
Reuse code
Reduce repetition
Improve performance

DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE procedure_name()
BEGIN
   SQL statements;
END //
DELIMITER ;

Example:
DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE show_students()
BEGIN
   SELECT * FROM student;
END //
DELIMITER ;

CALL show_students(); - #Call the Procedure


Types of Parameters
IN → Input value
OUT → Output value
IN OUT → Both input and output

Procedure with IN Parameter
IN parameter means input value is passed to the procedure.

EXAMPLE:
DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE get_student_by_id(IN sid INT)
BEGIN
   SELECT * FROM student WHERE id = sid;
END //
DELIMITER ;

Call:- CALL get_student_by_id(1);

Procedure with OUT Parameter

OUT parameter is used to return a value from the procedure.
It sends data from the procedure to user.

EXAMPLE:

DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE student_count(OUT total INT)
BEGIN
    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO total
    FROM students;
END //

DELIMITER ;

CALL student_count(@result);
SELECT @result;

Procedure with INOUT Parameter

INOUT parameter works as both input and output.
Value is sent to procedure
Procedure modifies the value
Modified value is returned back

DELIMITER //
CREATE PROCEDURE add_marks(INOUT extra INT)
BEGIN
    UPDATE students
    SET marks = marks + extra;
END //
DELIMITER ;

SET @bonus = 5;      - Creating a variable:
CALL add_marks(@bonus); - Call procedure 


DROP PROCEDURE procedure_name;
Drop  procedure if exists procedure_name;


