97. Triggers
A Trigger is a special type of stored procedure that automatically executes (fires) when a specific event occurs on a table or view.
Triggers are mainly used to maintain data integrity, and track changes in tables.

Triggers are executed automatically when one of the following events occurs:
INSERT – When a new row is added.
UPDATE – When an existing row is modified.
DELETE – When a row is removed.

Syntax:
CREATE TRIGGER trigger_name
BEFORE | AFTER
INSERT | UPDATE | DELETE
ON table_name
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
   SQL statements;
END;


AFTER triggers run after successful completion

BEFORE triggers run before an operation  used  to validate/modify data before storing it.
Company rule: Salary cannot be negative.

CREATE TRIGGER check_salary
BEFORE INSERT
ON employees
FOR EACH ROW
SET NEW.salary = ABS(NEW.salary);

User Inserts

INSERT INTO employees
VALUES(1,'Ravi',-50000);
What Happens?

Before data enters the table:
NEW.salary = ABS(-50000)

becomes
50000

Output
employees table
emp_id	name	  salary
  1	    Ravi   	50000


AFTER Trigger Example

Company wants to keep track of salary changes.
Tables:

employees
emp_id	salary
1      	50000

salary_log
old_salary	new_salary

CREATE TRIGGER log_salary
AFTER UPDATE
ON employees
FOR EACH ROW
INSERT INTO salary_log
VALUES(OLD.salary, NEW.salary);

User Updates Salary

UPDATE employees
SET salary = 70000
WHERE emp_id = 1;

What Happens?
Salary changes in employees table
AFTER trigger runs
Change is recorded in salary_log

Output

employees
emp_id	salary
  1	    70000

salary_log
old_salary	new_salary
50000	         70000


